WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.900
Function calling.

2
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:06.767
This is the atom of all tool use, and the loop every agent framework wraps.

3
00:00:07.967 --> 00:00:09.233
Here's the key idea.

4
00:00:09.533 --> 00:00:12.454
You describe your functions as JSON schemas.

5
00:00:12.454 --> 00:00:17.433
The model replies with a function name and JSON arguments instead of prose.

6
00:00:17.733 --> 00:00:19.850
Your code runs the real function.

7
00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:21.133
The model only asks.

8
00:00:22.333 --> 00:00:23.933
Here is the full round trip.

9
00:00:24.233 --> 00:00:26.500
You send the request with your tool schemas.

10
00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:30.800
The model decides a tool would help, and responds with a structured call.

11
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:33.867
Your code parses it and runs the real function.

12
00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:36.567
The result goes back as a new tool message.

13
00:00:36.867 --> 00:00:39.300
And the model folds it into its final answer.

14
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:41.967
Let's watch the messages.

15
00:00:42.267 --> 00:00:44.133
The user asks about asteroids.

16
00:00:44.433 --> 00:00:48.367
The model answers with a call to get neo feed, with this week's dates.

17
00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:51.533
Our code returns the JSON as a tool message.

18
00:00:51.833 --> 00:00:54.533
Then the model summarizes it in plain language.

19
00:00:55.733 --> 00:00:57.067
Here's the loop in code.

20
00:00:57.367 --> 00:01:00.167
We send the messages together with the tool schemas.

21
00:01:00.467 --> 00:01:03.767
We parse the call, and we run the real function ourselves.

22
00:01:04.067 --> 00:01:06.467
Then we append the result and request again.

23
00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:08.862
One common mistake.

24
00:01:08.862 --> 00:01:13.767
When two tools both mention events, vague descriptions cause wrong-tool calls.

25
00:01:14.067 --> 00:01:18.633
The fix is sharper descriptions, because the model chooses tools by reading them.

26
00:01:19.833 --> 00:01:22.467
This is the Orbital Research Agent's first tool.

27
00:01:22.767 --> 00:01:25.033
A user asks about nearby asteroids.

28
00:01:25.333 --> 00:01:29.767
The agent emits the call, and your code queries NASA's NEO API.

29
00:01:30.067 --> 00:01:34.700
Then the model turns the JSON into three named objects with miss distances.

30
00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:36.723
Let's recap.

31
00:01:36.723 --> 00:01:39.467
Describe your functions as JSON schemas.

32
00:01:39.767 --> 00:01:41.133
The model emits a call.

33
00:01:41.133 --> 00:01:42.500
It never runs anything.

34
00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:45.400
Your code executes and returns the result.

35
00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:49.145
Every agent is this loop with control flow around it.

36
00:01:49.145 --> 00:01:50.900
Next up: structured output.
